The rear camera's minimum exposure depends on binning, and with 4 the frame comes out flat

The driver declared a minimum exposure of 4 for every mode. With that value, the
four-by-four binned modes do not produce an image: they produce a FLAT, HIGH
field with nothing in it.

MEASURED with the scene in the dark and the gain at minimum, across all FIVE
modes, with both controls verified before each capture (ten-bit values):

    mode         binning    exp=4 and exp=5            exp>=6
    2312x1304      4x4      median 733, min 526        median 64
    1920x1080      4x4      median 733, min 545        median 64
    3840x2160      2x2      median 64                  median 64
    4624x3472      2x2      median 64                  median 64
    9248x6944      none     median 64                  median 64

So ONLY the two 4x4 binned modes break, and they do so below 6 exactly. The
boundary was bracketed by testing 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 12.

A median of 64 is the sensor's black pedestal, which is what should come out with
the scene in the dark. A flat 733 is neither a dark image nor a blown-out image:
it is a field with no information.

THIS MATTERS because the default mode is one of the affected ones, and because
libcamera's automatic exposure control drops down to whatever minimum the driver
declares as soon as there is light. So the rear camera went white in sunlight.

THE SENSOR DOES NOT DECLARE IT, and it is worth saying so rather than glossing
over it. Its own limit register was read out:

    0x1004 COARSE_INTEGRATION_TIME_MIN         = 0
    0x1006 COARSE_INTEGRATION_TIME_MAX_MARGIN  = 48

The margin does check out -it is exactly the one the driver was already using,
and that is why the maximum exposure of the 2312x1304 mode comes out as
1680-48 = 1632-, which says the I2C read is good and that the 0 for the minimum
is a real 0. So the 6 comes from the measurement and from nothing else.

A MEASUREMENT THAT HAD TO BE THROWN AWAY
----------------------------------------
The first run of the second mode gave absurd numbers -minimums at 0, maximums at
1023 and convincing medians- and I was about to conclude that the fault did not
depend on binning. It was the phone's /tmp being FULL: an earlier test had
requested 3000 frames to file and had written 2.7 GB, so the captures came out
truncated and the analysis was reading garbage. The tool now carries a free-space
guard, because a full disk produces data that looks good.
